From d6f7758819a47b617bcd58c9cbc362e953ddf142 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tesseract XRT port <jwkim@limagin.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 11:11:35 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 04/19] xocl: adapt subdev platform drivers to void remove()
 (Linux 6.11)

Mainline commit 0edb555a65d1 ("platform: Make platform_driver::remove()
return void", v6.11) changed the type of platform_driver::remove. All
57 subdev drivers still pass an int-returning function, which is an
incompatible-pointer-types error on 6.11+.

Most of these functions return -EINVAL when the subdev has no driver
data, and several are also called directly from their probe error
paths, so they are left untouched. xocl_drv.h gains two macros:

  XOCL_DEFINE_PLATFORM_REMOVE(fn)  emitted after fn in each subdev
  XOCL_PLATFORM_REMOVE(fn)         used as ".remove = ..."

Guard: LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(6, 11, 0). Before 6.11 the
first macro expands to nothing and the second to fn, so the compiled
code is identical to the original. Checked against mainline v6.10 and
v6.11 <linux/platform_device.h> and the 6.17.0-35 / 7.0.0-x headers.

Equivalence: on <= 6.10 the platform core called remove() and, on a
non-zero return, printed "remove callback returned a non-zero value.
This will be ignored." On 6.11+ the core only calls a void remove(), so
the generated fn_void() wrapper calls fn() and prints the same warning
with the same device, keeping dmesg output identical. Direct calls of
fn() from probe error paths still reach the int function and, as
before, print nothing extra.

Upstream: XRT commit 04350f685 renames each function to __fn and adds a
void wrapper that drops the return value (and the warning); this patch
keeps the warning and leaves every remove function and its callers
unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01SpY5QiU1d7kAe8YJM35EvA
